WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.541
Meet Tabby, a browser assistant that helps people organize their tabs and return to the

00:00:05.541 --> 00:00:06.509
materials they need.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.801
We open tabs to compare ideas, plan a trip, or collect references.

00:00:12.801 --> 00:00:14.509
Then another task interrupts us.

00:00:14.509 --> 00:00:20.319
The tabs stay open, because they hold useful materials and remind us of work we have not

00:00:20.319 --> 00:00:20.661
finished.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:28.336
Keeping everything open makes it harder to find the right page.

00:00:28.336 --> 00:00:31.641
But closing everything can feel like losing the task itself.

00:00:31.641 --> 00:00:34.616
The problem is not simply the number of tabs.

00:00:34.616 --> 00:00:37.260
It is keeping useful context while reducing clutter.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:46.019
What if an agent could help with both sides?

00:00:46.019 --> 00:00:49.338
Tabby connects task based organization with purpose based retrieval.

00:00:49.338 --> 00:00:54.870
It saves task names, links, and notes, then uses that context when the user returns.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.533
Here is the working prototype.

00:00:58.533 --> 00:01:02.565
On the web, users can paste links or load sample data.

00:01:02.565 --> 00:01:06.231
The Chrome extension reads tabs from the current browser window.

00:01:06.231 --> 00:01:09.896
In this example, the materials cover research, travel, and design.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.116
The user describes what these tabs are for and requests organization.

00:01:19.116 --> 00:01:23.933
The model proposes task groups using titles, addresses, and existing saved tasks.

00:01:23.933 --> 00:01:25.538
These are editable suggestions.

00:01:25.538 --> 00:01:31.158
The user can move tabs, change a group's name, or choose an existing task.

00:01:31.158 --> 00:01:33.567
Ambiguous materials remain available for review.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.468
A short note captures why these materials matter.

00:01:39.468 --> 00:01:43.273
The user then chooses to save only, or save and close.

00:01:43.273 --> 00:01:45.695
The web demo clears its working list.

00:01:45.695 --> 00:01:49.154
The extension closes the selected browser tabs after saving them.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:57.396
Later, the user returns with a purpose, rather than an exact page title.

00:01:57.396 --> 00:02:00.647
A vague request leads to a short clarifying question.

00:02:00.647 --> 00:02:06.065
Once the purpose is clear, Tabby connects it to saved tasks and recommends relevant tabs.

00:02:06.065 --> 00:02:09.678
Here, the goal is preparing a presentation about tab management.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:15.891
The first recommendation includes papers and implementation documents.

00:02:15.891 --> 00:02:19.082
The user can narrow it: exclude developer documentation.

00:02:19.082 --> 00:02:22.671
Tabby keeps the recent conversation and updates the candidates.

00:02:22.671 --> 00:02:25.862
Simple filtering can happen without another model call.

00:02:25.862 --> 00:02:29.452
The user stays in control of the final selection.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:33.808
The user selects the remaining tabs and reopens them.

00:02:33.808 --> 00:02:37.261
In the web prototype, they return to the working list.

00:02:37.261 --> 00:02:40.023
In Chrome, the extension opens the actual pages.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:47.760
Behind the interaction, the interface sends the request and relevant context to the

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:48.149
server.

00:02:48.149 --> 00:02:52.042
The model returns groups or recommendations in a structured format.

00:02:52.042 --> 00:02:55.156
Tabby checks the referenced tabs before showing them.

00:02:55.156 --> 00:02:57.491
User replies refine the next decision.

00:02:57.491 --> 00:03:02.551
Saving and browser actions happen after user selection, not automatically from the model's

00:03:02.551 --> 00:03:02.940
suggestion.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.570
Three people tried the prototype.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:11.804
Their feedback was generally positive, but they did not prefer agent assistance in every

00:03:11.804 --> 00:03:12.178
situation.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:20.330
With few tabs, or a clearly known target, the agent was unnecessary and could add noise.

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:21.738
Direct access still matters.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:28.206
Tabby helps people return to their work, with the tabs they need.
